WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.877
Welcome to Part 2 of
the technical deep dive

2
00:00:02.877 --> 00:00:05.625
on attribute-driven migration to Entra ID.

3
00:00:05.625 --> 00:00:09.275
Part 1 covered the model and
the HR track, the people.

4
00:00:09.275 --> 00:00:14.259
This part takes the other three tracks:
end-user devices through Intune,

5
00:00:14.259 --> 00:00:18.230
servers through Azure Arc,
and the non-person identities,

6
00:00:18.230 --> 00:00:21.187
service accounts, workloads and AI agents.

7
00:00:21.187 --> 00:00:24.726
Then it runs the migration,
from cutover to the checklist.

8
00:00:24.726 --> 00:00:31.150
Microsoft facts were checked on October
29, 2026, and design guidance is labeled.

9
00:00:31.150 --> 00:00:33.080
First, a quick recap.

10
00:00:36.830 --> 00:00:39.380
Two things from Part 1
carry everything here.

11
00:00:39.380 --> 00:00:41.230
First, the four tracks.

12
00:00:41.230 --> 00:00:45.717
People get their attributes from HR,
devices from device provisioning,

13
00:00:45.717 --> 00:00:48.911
servers from Arc onboarding
tags and Azure Policy,

14
00:00:48.911 --> 00:00:53.017
and non-person identities from
their owner's record, never from HR.

15
00:00:53.017 --> 00:00:55.730
Second, the store table, reduced.

16
00:00:55.730 --> 00:00:59.041
Extension attributes drive
user and device rules,

17
00:00:59.041 --> 00:01:04.243
custom security attributes feed
Conditional Access for applications but not

18
00:01:04.243 --> 00:01:09.130
dynamic rules, Intune's inventory feeds
filters, and Azure tags feed Policy.

19
00:01:09.130 --> 00:01:11.205
Part 1 covered the HR track.

20
00:01:11.205 --> 00:01:12.840
Now the computers.

21
00:01:16.590 --> 00:01:20.343
Module 1 is the Intune and Arc
tracks: what each tool covers,

22
00:01:20.343 --> 00:01:25.035
how devices and servers get attributes
that policy reads, the dangers of

23
00:01:25.035 --> 00:01:30.090
dynamic device groups, and a strategy
for whatever neither tool reaches.

24
00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:36.325
Now the Intune track: end-user devices,

25
00:01:36.325 --> 00:01:40.012
whose attributes come from
device provisioning, not HR.

26
00:01:40.012 --> 00:01:43.940
Start with what Intune covers,
because it's not just Windows.

27
00:01:43.940 --> 00:01:48.863
It manages Windows 11, Cloud PCs
and Azure Virtual Desktop, Macs,

28
00:01:48.863 --> 00:01:54.165
iPhones and iPads, Android in several
modes, and some Linux desktops.

29
00:01:54.165 --> 00:01:57.740
ChromeOS is limited:
Intune sees the inventory,

30
00:01:57.740 --> 00:02:00.490
but policy stays in Google's console.

31
00:02:00.490 --> 00:02:05.062
Old Windows releases and Windows
Server aren't Intune platforms,

32
00:02:05.062 --> 00:02:08.703
and neither are printers,
network gear or OT devices.

33
00:02:08.703 --> 00:02:13.228
So Intune does manage non-Microsoft
platforms, within limits.

34
00:02:13.228 --> 00:02:15.080
Now, how it targets them.

35
00:02:18.830 --> 00:02:23.155
This slide sets the default for Intune
targeting, so it's worth getting right.

36
00:02:23.155 --> 00:02:26.705
By default each policy goes
to a dynamic device group.

37
00:02:26.705 --> 00:02:29.810
Microsoft's current
guidance, checked October 29,

38
00:02:29.810 --> 00:02:33.369
is to prefer assignment filters
where they meet the need,

39
00:02:33.369 --> 00:02:36.702
because dynamic device
groups add processing delay,

40
00:02:36.702 --> 00:02:40.867
and the Federal Modernization
Workbook's Book 05 now says the same.

41
00:02:40.867 --> 00:02:45.255
Send user settings to user groups
and device settings to device groups.

42
00:02:45.255 --> 00:02:48.534
The exception is tier and
role: filters can't read

43
00:02:48.534 --> 00:02:52.468
device extension attributes,
so those stay in group rules.

44
00:02:52.468 --> 00:02:57.168
And underneath it all, a device rule
can never read its owner's attributes.

45
00:02:57.168 --> 00:03:00.120
Next, how device attributes get written.

46
00:03:03.870 --> 00:03:08.492
Devices are attribute-driven too, but
only through their own attributes,

47
00:03:08.492 --> 00:03:11.420
the device rows of the
store table in the recap.

48
00:03:11.420 --> 00:03:16.600
Dynamic device groups read things like
ownership, trust type and enrollment profile,

49
00:03:16.600 --> 00:03:19.883
and Autopilot devices carry
markers a rule can select.

50
00:03:19.883 --> 00:03:23.282
Filters read Intune's own
inventory at check-in.

51
00:03:23.282 --> 00:03:25.257
The highpoint is the red panel.

52
00:03:25.257 --> 00:03:29.515
Writing a device's extension attributes
needs the right Graph permission,

53
00:03:29.515 --> 00:03:32.468
and it depends on the call:
a signed-in administrator

54
00:03:32.468 --> 00:03:35.420
uses one permission, an
unattended service another.

55
00:03:35.420 --> 00:03:38.970
Book 05's sample now
uses the signed-in one.

56
00:03:38.970 --> 00:03:42.330
Then, the dangers of
dynamic device groups.

57
00:03:46.080 --> 00:03:48.858
Dynamic device groups
have their own dangers,

58
00:03:48.858 --> 00:03:51.855
and a mistake here reaches
the whole fleet at once.

59
00:03:51.855 --> 00:03:55.705
Only the device provisioning service
should write device attributes.

60
00:03:55.705 --> 00:04:00.119
A wrong or reused Autopilot group
tag puts a device in the wrong group,

61
00:04:00.119 --> 00:04:02.055
so check it before enrollment.

62
00:04:02.055 --> 00:04:04.133
Device rules can't read their owner,

63
00:04:04.133 --> 00:04:08.792
and stale or duplicate device objects
keep matching until you reconcile them.

64
00:04:08.792 --> 00:04:11.496
For time-sensitive policy, use filters,

65
00:04:11.496 --> 00:04:15.130
and never use a dynamic
device group as an exclusion.

66
00:04:15.130 --> 00:04:20.318
Pilot rings, one owning policy per setting,
and watching the deltas finish the list.

67
00:04:20.318 --> 00:04:22.230
Now, the servers.

68
00:04:25.980 --> 00:04:28.042
Now the Arc track: servers.

69
00:04:28.042 --> 00:04:29.905
Arc isn't just for Windows either.

70
00:04:29.905 --> 00:04:36.107
It covers Windows Server 2016 through
2025, a long list of Linux distributions,

71
00:04:36.107 --> 00:04:39.542
physical servers and
VMs on any hypervisor,

72
00:04:39.542 --> 00:04:44.217
VMware through vCenter, and
AWS and Google Cloud instances.

73
00:04:44.217 --> 00:04:50.406
Two dates matter: Windows Server 2012
stops getting new agents in November 2026,

74
00:04:50.406 --> 00:04:53.406
and the SCVMM integration is retiring.

75
00:04:53.406 --> 00:04:57.955
Azure VMs don't need Arc at all,
because they're already Azure resources.

76
00:04:57.955 --> 00:05:02.780
And Unix, mainframes, network devices
and sealed appliances are out.

77
00:05:02.780 --> 00:05:06.000
So here's how Arc makes a
server attribute-driven.

78
00:05:09.750 --> 00:05:13.728
Arc servers matter because
they're the on-premises servers

79
00:05:13.728 --> 00:05:16.975
the organizational unit, or
O U, tree used to scope.

80
00:05:16.975 --> 00:05:22.387
Once connected, a server becomes an Azure
resource, and tags become its attributes.

81
00:05:22.387 --> 00:05:26.287
You set them at onboarding or
later, Azure Policy can test them

82
00:05:26.287 --> 00:05:30.263
and even inherit them, and Resource
Graph queries them at scale.

83
00:05:30.263 --> 00:05:32.200
One boundary to state plainly.

84
00:05:32.200 --> 00:05:36.882
Azure's attribute-based access conditions
apply only to storage data today,

85
00:05:36.882 --> 00:05:40.375
so Microsoft doesn't document
them for managing servers.

86
00:05:40.375 --> 00:05:42.962
Role assignments at the
right scope do that job.

87
00:05:42.962 --> 00:05:46.120
Next, the devices neither tool covers.

88
00:05:49.870 --> 00:05:52.207
So what about everything
neither tool reaches?

89
00:05:52.207 --> 00:05:57.320
It still needs one source of truth, and
the program's design gives it four tiers.

90
00:05:57.320 --> 00:05:59.663
Tier one is managed by Intune or Arc.

91
00:05:59.663 --> 00:06:04.188
Tier two is managed by its own platform
tool, like the Google console,

92
00:06:04.188 --> 00:06:07.985
a network controller or the
mainframe's security system,

93
00:06:07.985 --> 00:06:11.783
feeding evidence to the C M D
B, which holds its attributes.

94
00:06:11.783 --> 00:06:17.420
Tier three is discovered and monitored
only, like printers, cameras and OT.

95
00:06:17.420 --> 00:06:19.845
Tier four is segmented or retired.

96
00:06:19.845 --> 00:06:22.347
That's how the inventory meets C M 8:

97
00:06:22.347 --> 00:06:27.082
every component counted once, and
a device in no tier is a finding.

98
00:06:27.082 --> 00:06:30.990
Now, the fourth track:
the non-person identities.

99
00:06:34.740 --> 00:06:37.302
Module 2 is the non-person track.

100
00:06:37.302 --> 00:06:39.987
Service accounts, service principals,

101
00:06:39.987 --> 00:06:45.442
workload identities and AI agents have no
HR record, so each needs a named owner,

102
00:06:45.442 --> 00:06:49.890
a credential that's managed or
expires, and a lifecycle that ends.

103
00:06:49.890 --> 00:06:54.050
Then the crosswalk across
every track, and PIV binding.

104
00:06:57.800 --> 00:07:01.287
The non-person track starts
with what AD already holds.

105
00:07:01.287 --> 00:07:06.187
Service accounts have no HR record, so
nobody's leave date ever turns them off,

106
00:07:06.187 --> 00:07:10.075
and that's why they're so often
over-privileged and forgotten.

107
00:07:10.075 --> 00:07:13.338
The table is Book 02's migration matrix.

108
00:07:13.338 --> 00:07:16.182
The rule is to get rid of stored secrets:

109
00:07:16.182 --> 00:07:22.403
a traditional account becomes an app
registration with a certificate or federated

110
00:07:22.403 --> 00:07:27.113
credential, or a managed identity
in Azure; on-premises Kerberos

111
00:07:27.113 --> 00:07:31.201
workloads go to a g M S A; and an
M S A upgrades to a g M S A first.

112
00:07:31.201 --> 00:07:33.614
Each one gets an owner
before it's touched,

113
00:07:33.614 --> 00:07:36.950
and an account nobody will
own is a retirement candidate.

114
00:07:36.950 --> 00:07:39.020
Next, the cloud side.

115
00:07:42.770 --> 00:07:45.970
The rest of the non-person
track lives in Entra ID,

116
00:07:45.970 --> 00:07:50.210
and the question for each identity
is the same: who answers for it,

117
00:07:50.210 --> 00:07:53.570
what credential does it
hold, and what ends its life.

118
00:07:53.570 --> 00:07:58.345
Service principals get two human owners
and no client secrets in production.

119
00:07:58.345 --> 00:08:03.145
Managed identities have credentials
nobody sees, and end with their resource.

120
00:08:03.145 --> 00:08:08.320
Workload identity federation trusts an
external issuer, so there's no secret at all.

121
00:08:08.320 --> 00:08:13.090
And AI agents get an agent identity
with a required sponsor; the program

122
00:08:13.090 --> 00:08:18.183
makes that sponsor an HR-mastered federal
employee, and expiry is the review.

123
00:08:18.183 --> 00:08:23.020
One caution: check what your cloud supports
before you plan on agent identities.

124
00:08:23.020 --> 00:08:26.340
Next, how attributes reach applications.

125
00:08:30.090 --> 00:08:33.577
Applications and service
principals are the partial answer.

126
00:08:33.577 --> 00:08:37.415
The table repeats the recap's
store table for these consumers.

127
00:08:37.415 --> 00:08:40.465
Extension attributes drive dynamic groups.

128
00:08:40.465 --> 00:08:45.412
Custom security attributes can't, but
Conditional Access can filter applications

129
00:08:45.412 --> 00:08:49.928
by them, and they suit sensitive values
that only specific roles should read.

130
00:08:49.928 --> 00:08:54.376
For service principals, workload
Conditional Access only blocks,

131
00:08:54.376 --> 00:08:59.552
applies only to direct assignment, and
needs the Workload I D Premium license.

132
00:08:59.552 --> 00:09:03.852
And Microsoft documents no dynamic
groups of service principals at all.

133
00:09:03.852 --> 00:09:07.202
So access codes stay in
the user extension slots.

134
00:09:07.202 --> 00:09:09.530
Here's everything on one page.

135
00:09:13.280 --> 00:09:16.463
This crosswalk is the
reference you'll come back to,

136
00:09:16.463 --> 00:09:19.798
the store table seen by object
type: every object type,

137
00:09:19.798 --> 00:09:24.042
the store its attributes live in,
who writes them and what reads them.

138
00:09:24.042 --> 00:09:27.455
Users, devices, Arc servers
and service principals

139
00:09:27.455 --> 00:09:30.867
each have their own stores
and their own consumers.

140
00:09:30.867 --> 00:09:32.842
The highpoint is the third column.

141
00:09:32.842 --> 00:09:36.548
Every row names one writer,
whether that's HR provisioning,

142
00:09:36.548 --> 00:09:40.793
device registration, the Arc agent
or an attribute administrator.

143
00:09:40.793 --> 00:09:44.161
That's the same single-writer
rule we set for the user schema,

144
00:09:44.161 --> 00:09:45.467
applied to everything.

145
00:09:45.467 --> 00:09:49.030
And one user row leads straight into PIV.

146
00:09:52.780 --> 00:09:57.605
PIV and CAC sign-in is attribute-driven
too, which is easy to miss.

147
00:09:57.605 --> 00:10:00.427
Entra ID finds the user
by matching a field

148
00:10:00.427 --> 00:10:03.330
on the certificate to
a field on the account.

149
00:10:03.330 --> 00:10:06.912
Some matches are low affinity,
like the name on the card,

150
00:10:06.912 --> 00:10:10.255
and some are high, tied to
one specific certificate.

151
00:10:10.255 --> 00:10:11.418
Two highpoints.

152
00:10:11.418 --> 00:10:16.050
Certificate-based sign-in is only as
strong as the weakest binding you allow,

153
00:10:16.050 --> 00:10:19.580
and the program's position is
high affinity in production.

154
00:10:19.580 --> 00:10:22.327
But switch the whole tenant
to high before every

155
00:10:22.327 --> 00:10:25.005
account's value is right,
and you lock people out.

156
00:10:25.005 --> 00:10:26.205
Pilot it first.

157
00:10:26.205 --> 00:10:28.380
So where does that value come from?

158
00:10:32.130 --> 00:10:36.942
The certificate user I Ds attribute,
the last row of the store table,

159
00:10:36.942 --> 00:10:41.580
holds the binding, so who writes it
matters as much as any HR field.

160
00:10:41.580 --> 00:10:46.855
For cloud-only users, a privileged
authentication administrator or Graph writes it.

161
00:10:46.855 --> 00:10:51.191
For synced users, only Entra
Connect can, from Active Directory,

162
00:10:51.191 --> 00:10:54.443
and Microsoft's page
doesn't mention Cloud Sync.

163
00:10:54.443 --> 00:10:59.002
HR supplies the person; the card
management system supplies the binding,

164
00:10:59.002 --> 00:11:01.206
because a reissued card changes it.

165
00:11:01.206 --> 00:11:03.150
After Active Directory is gone,

166
00:11:03.150 --> 00:11:06.293
the plan is for the card
system to write it directly.

167
00:11:06.293 --> 00:11:09.705
Be clear that this is design
guidance, and unverified.

168
00:11:09.705 --> 00:11:13.030
It's an open pilot,
workbook issue one thirty.

169
00:11:13.030 --> 00:11:14.430
Now let's run it.

170
00:11:18.180 --> 00:11:20.417
Module 3 runs the migration.

171
00:11:20.417 --> 00:11:25.323
We'll follow one analyst through a reassignment
with no ticket, cut over in phases,

172
00:11:25.323 --> 00:11:29.789
keep the attributes true with drift
detection, and finish with the limits,

173
00:11:29.789 --> 00:11:33.070
the checklist and the
decisions for every track.

174
00:11:36.820 --> 00:11:40.795
Here's the model working on one
person, which is the best test of it.

175
00:11:40.795 --> 00:11:45.620
She's a budget analyst in a finance
division, still mastered in Active Directory.

176
00:11:45.620 --> 00:11:51.120
Her HR record becomes a handful of coded
attributes, and every mechanism reads them.

177
00:11:51.120 --> 00:11:55.995
Her organization code puts her in the right
groups, and in a database reader group.

178
00:11:55.995 --> 00:12:00.220
Her division puts her in the unit
whose help desk can reset her password.

179
00:12:00.220 --> 00:12:03.070
Her worker type puts her
in the employee persona.

180
00:12:03.070 --> 00:12:06.833
And her laptop is governed by
its own attributes, not hers.

181
00:12:06.833 --> 00:12:09.169
Nobody assigned any of it by hand.

182
00:12:09.169 --> 00:12:11.080
Now HR moves her.

183
00:12:14.830 --> 00:12:16.955
This is why the whole design exists.

184
00:12:16.955 --> 00:12:21.099
HR records her move to accounts
payable as a personnel action,

185
00:12:21.099 --> 00:12:22.805
and nobody opens a ticket.

186
00:12:22.805 --> 00:12:27.430
The record flows through the middleware
to Active Directory and up through sync.

187
00:12:27.430 --> 00:12:29.662
Her groups and units recompute,

188
00:12:29.662 --> 00:12:33.268
usually within hours, but it
can take more than a day.

189
00:12:33.268 --> 00:12:37.981
A workflow swaps her access packages
and starts a review of what's left.

190
00:12:37.981 --> 00:12:41.618
Her laptop stays put, because
device rules can't see her.

191
00:12:41.618 --> 00:12:44.767
And a drift scan checks
the result field by field.

192
00:12:44.767 --> 00:12:47.170
Next, cutting over at scale.

193
00:12:50.920 --> 00:12:56.332
Stage seven is a runbook, not a flag day,
and that's what keeps rollback possible.

194
00:12:56.332 --> 00:12:58.892
Waves are defined by attribute values,

195
00:12:58.892 --> 00:13:02.171
so each wave is a query
whose groups already exist.

196
00:13:02.171 --> 00:13:06.720
Start with a pilot division, and keep
each one drift-clean before the next.

197
00:13:06.720 --> 00:13:11.183
Do leaver automation first, because
revocation is where the risk is.

198
00:13:11.183 --> 00:13:14.832
The gate is the strip across
the top: a signed report,

199
00:13:14.832 --> 00:13:20.132
a dry run with writes off, then seven
days in which every change traces to HR.

200
00:13:20.132 --> 00:13:22.295
Only then does the ticket path close.

201
00:13:22.295 --> 00:13:25.933
And disable, never delete,
so you can always go back.

202
00:13:25.933 --> 00:13:27.390
Then, drift.

203
00:13:31.140 --> 00:13:34.763
Stage eight keeps the
statements true after cutover;

204
00:13:34.763 --> 00:13:37.315
without it, everything slowly decays.

205
00:13:37.315 --> 00:13:41.262
Two comparisons run on a schedule:
HR against the directory,

206
00:13:41.262 --> 00:13:44.565
and the unit and role
registry against the tenant.

207
00:13:44.565 --> 00:13:49.648
Every finding goes to the CISO and is
fixed at the source, in the HR record or

208
00:13:49.648 --> 00:13:54.977
the rule, never in the copy, because the
next cycle would just overwrite your fix.

209
00:13:54.977 --> 00:13:57.377
The callout is the one to act on today.

210
00:13:57.377 --> 00:14:00.207
Entra's logs don't last
nearly as long as federal

211
00:14:00.207 --> 00:14:03.253
records rules require, and
they aren't retroactive.

212
00:14:03.253 --> 00:14:04.741
Route them out from day one.

213
00:14:04.741 --> 00:14:06.550
Now, the limits.

214
00:14:10.300 --> 00:14:13.466
Administrative units are
the delegation replacement,

215
00:14:13.466 --> 00:14:16.287
so their limits shape the
whole delegation model.

216
00:14:16.287 --> 00:14:18.915
They don't nest, so the
tree becomes a flat set,

217
00:14:18.915 --> 00:14:22.712
and a role covering several units
gets one assignment per unit.

218
00:14:22.712 --> 00:14:25.888
They limit who can change
members, not who can see them.

219
00:14:25.888 --> 00:14:28.312
Only some roles can be scoped to a unit.

220
00:14:28.312 --> 00:14:32.560
Dynamic units work in G C C,
but not in G C C High or D O D,

221
00:14:32.560 --> 00:14:36.187
where an automation job
has to keep them up.

222
00:14:36.187 --> 00:14:40.510
And restricting a unit takes its
members out of governance automation,

223
00:14:40.510 --> 00:14:43.513
so restriction is a deliberate
choice, default no.

224
00:14:43.513 --> 00:14:46.000
Next, Lifecycle Workflows.

225
00:14:49.750 --> 00:14:55.025
Lifecycle Workflows carry joiner, mover
and leaver, so it helps to know the pieces.

226
00:14:55.025 --> 00:14:58.987
A workflow has a trigger, a
scope rule and a list of tasks.

227
00:14:58.987 --> 00:15:03.037
Custom task extensions call a
Logic App to reach other systems.

228
00:15:03.037 --> 00:15:07.801
Scheduled runs happen on one tenant
interval, every three hours by default.

229
00:15:07.801 --> 00:15:13.037
The limits are a hundred workflows, twenty-five
tasks each and a hundred extensions.

230
00:15:13.037 --> 00:15:15.450
Two behaviors deserve the red box.

231
00:15:15.450 --> 00:15:20.293
Execution limits can quarantine a scheduled
run that would touch too many people,

232
00:15:20.293 --> 00:15:22.714
but an on-demand run skips that guardrail,

233
00:15:22.714 --> 00:15:25.563
and it ignores the workflow's
own conditions too.

234
00:15:25.563 --> 00:15:27.700
So what could break at our size?

235
00:15:31.450 --> 00:15:35.309
At an agency our size, the
limits bite in predictable ways,

236
00:15:35.309 --> 00:15:38.675
and this is the program's
design guidance for each.

237
00:15:38.675 --> 00:15:42.025
A workflow per organization
runs out of a hundred fast,

238
00:15:42.025 --> 00:15:45.300
so build a few general
workflows that branch on codes.

239
00:15:45.300 --> 00:15:48.588
Build one reusable Logic
App per integration.

240
00:15:48.588 --> 00:15:52.106
Treat every on-demand run as
a change with a second person,

241
00:15:52.106 --> 00:15:54.801
because it can disable
the wrong ten people.

242
00:15:54.801 --> 00:15:57.599
Size execution limits so a reorganization

243
00:15:57.599 --> 00:16:00.475
gets quarantined until
someone approves it.

244
00:16:00.475 --> 00:16:05.287
For urgent terminations, don't wait
for the schedule: disable and revoke.

245
00:16:05.287 --> 00:16:08.841
And until PIV revoke and
device retire are automated,

246
00:16:08.841 --> 00:16:11.263
keep a leaver checklist with owners.

247
00:16:11.263 --> 00:16:13.560
Next, what still needs AD.

248
00:16:17.310 --> 00:16:19.734
Hybrid lasts longer than anyone plans,

249
00:16:19.734 --> 00:16:23.597
so it helps to name exactly what
still needs Active Directory.

250
00:16:23.597 --> 00:16:26.198
Synced users' attributes
are still written there.

251
00:16:26.198 --> 00:16:29.123
Kerberos applications
need groups written back.

252
00:16:29.123 --> 00:16:33.033
D N S, D H C P and legacy
apps keep the forest alive,

253
00:16:33.033 --> 00:16:37.973
and Windows logins to SQL
Server skip Conditional Access.

254
00:16:37.973 --> 00:16:41.110
Hybrid-joined devices still
need some domain policy.

255
00:16:41.110 --> 00:16:45.274
Moving a population's source of
authority has firm entry checks,

256
00:16:45.274 --> 00:16:48.810
and whether that move works
in G C C is still unverified.

257
00:16:48.810 --> 00:16:53.872
So retiring AD means clearing a list of
named dependencies, not reaching a date.

258
00:16:53.872 --> 00:16:55.550
Here's the checklist.

259
00:16:59.300 --> 00:17:03.190
The checklist turns the nine stages
into something you can tick off,

260
00:17:03.190 --> 00:17:04.787
and that's its whole purpose.

261
00:17:04.787 --> 00:17:07.444
There's one row per stage, plus PIV,

262
00:17:07.444 --> 00:17:11.675
and one each for the Intune,
Arc and non-person tracks.

263
00:17:11.675 --> 00:17:13.250
A few are easy to miss.

264
00:17:13.250 --> 00:17:15.962
Export site links, not just O U links.

265
00:17:15.962 --> 00:17:18.512
Match accounts before the first full sync.

266
00:17:18.512 --> 00:17:22.450
Write every access rule on
codes, and don't use member-of.

267
00:17:22.450 --> 00:17:25.562
Decide unit restriction
before the unit exists.

268
00:17:25.562 --> 00:17:27.600
Pair every grant with a block.

269
00:17:27.600 --> 00:17:31.850
Prove seven days of sole-source
changes before closing the ticket path.

270
00:17:31.850 --> 00:17:33.900
And route logs out from the first day.

271
00:17:33.900 --> 00:17:35.980
Finally, who decides.

272
00:17:39.730 --> 00:17:41.729
Every decision here has one owner,

273
00:17:41.729 --> 00:17:44.655
and that's what keeps the
migration from stalling.

274
00:17:44.655 --> 00:17:47.541
Identity engineering owns the plumbing,

275
00:17:47.541 --> 00:17:50.767
the delegation model and
each wave's go or no-go.

276
00:17:50.767 --> 00:17:55.093
The ICAM governance body owns the
authority map and the person key.

277
00:17:55.093 --> 00:17:59.043
HR owns the codes, the data
architect the schema, and application

278
00:17:59.043 --> 00:18:03.367
owners their own access rules and
their service accounts' conversion.

279
00:18:03.367 --> 00:18:06.768
The CISO owns the controls
and the drift findings.

280
00:18:06.768 --> 00:18:12.234
And you can start Monday without new tools:
export the tree, name the HR feed early,

281
00:18:12.234 --> 00:18:16.955
populate the person key, remove other
writers, and route your logs out.

282
00:18:16.955 --> 00:18:19.893
Make the attributes
true, and keep them true.

283
00:18:19.893 --> 00:18:20.720
Thank you.
